i’m 36 years old, a self-identified trans woman, six years into transition, and unemployed. At this point, staying with my partner of over a year and a half is out of convenience. My fear of being alone permeates my thoughts. I only know what life is like inside my own head. i am on the brink of total internal collapse. The passion i have for my partner is long gone. i’ll call them Gus. 

Gus and i have been seeing each for a few days at a time every few weeks. Before i met Gus i was unmotivated and miserable since my transition started. My motivation for companionship stemmed from deep loneliness. Sure, i hooked up with people a few times using queer friendly dating apps. None felt meaningful. Hardly any felt fun or even satisfying. Usually i was reduced to a wet hole in one form or another, quickly cast aside when the fun was over. Staying active on dating apps led me to find Gus in a moment of openness and vulnerability that at the time felt new to me. 

My vulnerability led me to choosing Gus out of 50 others who contacted me regarding a post i made in August 2021, titled “nude beach revelations”, that explained why lying on a nude beach as a trans woman suddenly transformed my self image. i’d never been so aware of my sexuality before. i never thought anyone could find me attractive. The dozens of people who eyed me up and down as i soaked up the sun that day made me realize how much i had lied to myself. i had hoped these open feelings would be mutual and reciprocal with my new choice of mate. i shortly found out that Gus wasn’t much help here.

 Gus had no motivation or drive outside of answering the burdens of existence, and only just barely. They had few people close to them so they kept me closer. Near the end that closeness had proven to be toxic. The expectation was always on me to provide. Provide what? EVERYTHING. It was always up to me where to go, what to do. Often Gus would use my opinions and choices to come to a decision in a play to make it seem like they were helping. Rarely would they volunteer an idea for us on their own. 

i felt burdened by Gus’ inability to come to any decision. We relied more heavily on my money though neither of us had any coming in. Besides, getting them interested in going anywhere outside of a restaurant or cafe to quickly pick up food and go was nearly impossible. The odd trip to Target was almost the only “exciting” thing we ever got to do when we went out.

On top of that, Gus was more content to dissociate and cast blame about their place in life than any other activity. Lucidity was possible but their mental ailments were many. That was a lifestyle i knew so much about. For nearly my entire life i felt like an outcast. i grew up as a boy but mainly made friends with girls. There was no emotional space for me at home since home life was dominated by violence of all kinds. My sibling, two years younger than me, made herself the center of attention in the worst ways possible. My parents hated each other. All i ever learned from them and from my environment was to hate myself. All that i could do was shrink my self and my needs. i stayed quiet. Absent. Alone.

Gus’ pain was familiar. The solitude, the ennui, the feeling of being adrift at sea felt like 

home. Over the course of our individual lives we had both chosen media as an escape. For Gus it was mainly in books. For me, video games. As we grew up, our loves became obsessions. We’d pore over every bit of lore in a franchise. We’d identify with characters, using their plight as a distraction in unhealthy coping mechanisms. We’d daydream of worlds we would never know. In Gus i had found a misplaced sense of comfort. So for a time, i stayed with them.
